; ======================================================================
;  hw_mod.pi3 - THE Pi 3's HALF OF THE DRIVER-MODULE CONTRACT.
;
;  REQUIRES, in this order, in the board file:
;      Anvil/Hal/seams.pbi                 the #SVCCAP_* vocabulary
;      RaspberryPi3/Board/memmap.pi3       the region enumeration
;
;  THIS BOARD DECLARES NO MODULE ARENA, AND SAYS SO RATHER THAN INVENTING
;  ONE. That is the whole content of this file and it is a deliberate
;  answer, not a stub left behind.
;
;  WHY NOT SIMPLY CARVE ONE. There is room - the payload window on a
;  stock 1 GB board is most of a gigabyte - and that is exactly why it
;  would be easy to get wrong. An arena is a region the monitor must
;  refuse writes to for ever after (memmap.pi3 would grow an eighth
;  region), it must be cache-synchronised before anything branches into
;  it, and the synchronisation must be PROVEN rather than assumed. This
;  monitor has just turned its own caches on for the first time on this
;  part; the maintenance instructions a module loader needs have never
;  run here in this tree. Declaring an arena now would mean the first
;  thing ever executed from it is also the first test of the cache
;  maintenance that made it safe, with no way to tell which of the two
;  failed.
;
;  WHAT THE REAL ANSWER IS, so the next lane does not have to derive it:
;  a fixed window above the A/B staging arena and below the payload
;  window's floor, declared in memmap.pi3 as an eighth region so that
;  HitsMonitor() refuses it; HwModCodeSync() implemented as a data-cache
;  clean by virtual address to the point of unification followed by an
;  instruction-cache invalidate over the same range; and a probe that
;  writes a known sequence, syncs it, branches into it and reports what
;  it found, run on silicon, BEFORE the loader is allowed to.
;
;  UNTIL THEN: HwModArenaBase() answers 0, ModArenaInit() refuses with
;  #MOD_ERR_NOARENA, the boot walk stays silent about it, and `mod` says
;  in one sentence that this board declares no arena. Everything else in
;  the loader - the container reader, the validator, the relocator, the
;  registry, the lifecycle and the console - compiles and links here
;  identically to the other boards, which is the build-closure claim this
;  file exists to keep true.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i HwModArenaBase()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModArenaBytes()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; No region of this board's map is the arena, so there is no index for
; ModArenaInit() to skip. -1 matches no region.
Procedure.i HwModArenaRegion()
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModStageAddr()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModStageBytes()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwModCodeSync(base, bytes) - REFUSES, and refusing is the correct
;  answer rather than a placeholder.
;
;  The loader calls this after it has written a module's bytes and before
;  anything branches into them, and it treats a non-1 answer as
;  #MOD_ERR_SYNC: the module is NOT initialised and the arena bytes are
;  cleared again. Answering 1 without performing the maintenance would
;  make the loader believe a cache state nobody established, which is the
;  one failure in this whole path that produces no message at all - a
;  branch into stale instruction lines.
;
;  This is where the argument in the header lands as code: there is no
;  arena, so nothing can be written into one, so this can never honestly
;  be reached - and if it is, the safe answer is the one that stops the
;  branch.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwModCodeSync(base.i, bytes.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DEVICES. None are declared, and that is the truth rather than an
;  omission: nothing on this part has been enumerated to the point where
;  a driver could be handed a block and told it is safe to read. The
;  hardware this board HAS that a module might one day own - the SD host,
;  the USB controller, the Ethernet adapter behind it, the radio - is
;  either compiled in or not yet written, and in neither case is there a
;  device record for a module to bind to.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwDevCount()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevIdAddr(i.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevSeam(i.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevToken(i.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure HwDevSay(i.i)
  Print("a device this board does not declare")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE CAPABILITY QUESTIONS. Both answer 0 for every id: this board
;  reaches no seam from the core through a module, and no piece of its
;  hardware has been established as present in a form a module could bind
;  to. A board that claimed otherwise would send an operator looking for
;  a module to load that could never bind to anything.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwSeamCore(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwSeamPossible(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
